For many non-native English users, so-called "difficult" (polysyllabic,
neo-Latinate or Greek-ish) words aren't that difficult because they may
know the words from their own language.  They are often international.
In opposition (this sounds awkward) to that, idioms are very
language-specific and take lifelong learning to master.  That's what I
think, anyway...

The Finnish form of "euthanasia" is "eutanasia" and that of "euphemism",
"eufemismi" although the latter word isn't commonly used at all.  The
more usual word would be "kiertoilmaus", ie. 'avoiding expression',
literally.

"Putting to sleep" can be expressed with the verb "nukuttaa", which can
also mean 'make someone (usually a child) sleep' or 'anesthesize' or,
used impersonally, 'feel sleepy'.

